dia in 1947 transformed the themes of the ghazal, reflecting the profound loss and displacement experienced by individuals."}], "places": [{"name": "Delhi, India", "role": "Cultural Center"}, {"name": "Istanbul, Turkey", "role": "Literary Hub"}, {"name": "Tehran, Iran", "role": "Historical Origin"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: ghazal", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "ghazal"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q281111", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ghazal"], "tags": ["music genre", "Music & Compositions", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "ghazal left a notable mark on Classical history, documented across 76 Wikipedia editions worldwide. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "The ghazal has significantly shaped poetic traditions in South Asia and the Middle East, influencing thousands of poets and writers and fostering cultural dialogues across regions.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-06-09T01:25:35Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#27177785068", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Form of poetry in south asia and the middle east, whose first and every even-numbered lines rhyme", "newValue": "The ghazal, a poetic form originating in the Arabic literary tradition, emerged prominently around the 6th century CE and has since evolved into a significant cultural artifact across South Asia and the Middle East. Characterized by its lyrical expression of love, loss, and mysticism, the ghazal comprises rhyming couplets with a refrain, captivating audiences with its musicality and emotional depth. Noteworthy poets such as Rumi, Ghalib, and Faiz Ahmed Faiz exemplified the ghazal's rich tradition, making it a crucial part of their literary legacies and a reflection of the sociopolitical landscapes of their times.

